  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following is **NOT** a root of the polynomial ${x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-21x+45?$\n\nI. $x=3$\n\nII. $x=4$\n\nIII. $x=5$",
  "worked_solution": "- Substituting $3$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3}-{3}^{2}-21(3)+45 & = \\\\ 27-9-63+45 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $3$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $4$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3}-{4}^{2}-21(4)+45 & = \\\\ 64-16-84+45 & = \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $5$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{3}-{5}^{2}-21(5)+45 & = \\\\ 125-25-105+45 & = \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $5$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $5$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the values that are *not* roots are $x=4$ and $x=5,$ so the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
